-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/cc2658e6-d981-557f-ba10-785227412b31
-- title:
--   Level automorphism carrying one chart point to another, q=2
-- statement:
--   Data. A prime $q$, assumed by `hq2` to be equal to $2$; a nonzero natural number $M'$ with $q \nmid M'$ (`hqM'`); and a prime $\ell_g$ with $\ell_g \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ (`hℓg12`) and $\ell_g \mid M'$ (`hℓgM'`).
--
--   Reduction data at a supersingular place. $A$ is a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with `A.LiesOverPrime q`, i.e. $q$ is a non-unit of $A$. The field $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}\,(\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A)\,M'$ is the subfield of the Laurent series field over the residue field of $A$ generated by the two series `jqModC` and `jqNModC … M'`; $W$ is a finite set of places of this field over $\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A$, and `hW` states that $W$ consists precisely of the members of `ssPlaces q M' (ResidueField A)`, that is, of those places $w$ which are rational (the structure map from the base field to the residue field of $w$ is surjective), satisfy the predicate `IsAffineGeomPlace`, and whose value `w.evalAt (jGeomGen …)` lies in `ssJSet q`. The hypothesis `hle` asserts the inclusion $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,M' \le \mathrm{fieldBar}\,q\,M'$ of intermediate fields of $\overline{\mathbb Q} \subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,\overline{\mathbb Q}$: the base change to $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ of the field generated over $\mathbb Q$ by the expansions $j(q^d)$ for $d \mid M'$ is contained in the base change to $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ of the function field of $X_H$ of level $q^2M'$, where $H = \mathrm{levelH}\,q\,M'$ is the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb Z/q)^\times$, i.e. the units congruent to $1$ modulo $q$.
--
--   $R_0$ is a `ConstantReduction` of $A$ from $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,M'$ to $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}\,(\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A)\,M'$: it consists of a valuation subring `R₀.integers` of the characteristic-zero field, a surjective ring homomorphism `R₀.residue` from it onto the characteristic-$p$ field whose kernel is the maximal ideal, a map `R₀.placeMap` on places, and the compatibilities recorded in the structure (an element of the base field lies in the integers iff it lies in $A$; the residue map restricted to $A$ is the residue map of $A$; every nonzero element becomes, after scaling by a constant, an integral element with nonzero residue; `placeMap` preserves degrees and transports divisors of functions with nonzero residue to divisors of their residues). The hypothesis `hR₀` states that for every Laurent series $y$ with coefficients in $A$ whose coefficientwise image in $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,\overline{\mathbb Q}$ lies in $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,M'$, that element belongs to `R₀.integers` and its $R_0$-residue, read as a Laurent series over $\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A$, is the coefficientwise reduction of $y$. Finally $s$ is an element of $W$, i.e. a supersingular place.
--
--   Local field data. $k_0$ is an intermediate field of $\mathbb Q \subseteq \overline{\mathbb Q}$ and $\pi_0 \in k_0$ an element lying in $A$ (`hπ`). Writing $A_0 = A \cap k_0$ for the comap of $A$ along $k_0 \to \overline{\mathbb Q}$, the hypotheses require: $A_0$ is a discrete valuation ring (`hdvr`) whose maximal ideal is generated by $\pi_0$ (`hunif`), $A_0$ is a henselian local ring (`hhens`), its residue field is algebraically closed (`hres`), and every element of $A$ is congruent, modulo the maximal ideal of $A$, to some element of $k_0$ lying in $A$ (`hκ`).
--
--   Roots of unity and the level field. $\xi \in k_0$ is a primitive $q$-th root of unity (`hξ`), and `hιξ` requires a ring homomorphism $\iota : k_0 \to \mathbb C$ with $\iota(\xi) = e^{2\pi i/q}$. The subgroup $H_1 \le (\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times$ is, by `hH₁`, the intersection of $\mathrm{levelH}\,q\,M'$ (units congruent to $1$ modulo $q$) with the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb Z/\ell_g)^\times$. The field $K$ is, by `hK`, the intermediate field $\mathrm{laurentBaseChange}\,k_0\,(\mathrm{xHFunctionField}\,(q^2M')\,H_1)$, the subfield of $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,k_0$ generated over $k_0$ by the coefficientwise image of the rational $q$-expansion field of $X_{H_1}(q^2M')$; it is regarded as an $A_0$-algebra compatibly with $k_0$. The element $j \in K$ is nonzero and its Laurent expansion is the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion `jq` of the modular invariant (`hj`).
--
--   Chart and the two points. Write $C = \mathrm{chartAlgFin}\,A_0\,K\,j$ for the $A_0$-subalgebra of $K$ consisting of the elements integral over $A_0[j]$. Then $y$ and $y'$ are ideals of $C$, both maximal (`hy`, `hy'`), each containing the image of $\pi_0$ under $A_0 \to C$ (`hπy`, `hπy'`).
--
--   The two lying-over hypotheses. The hypotheses `hover` and `hover'` are the same condition for $y$ and for $y'$ respectively: for every $g$ in $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldFull}\,M'$ (a Laurent series over $\mathbb Q$) whose image in $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,M'$ lies in `R₀.integers`, such that $g$ has non-negative order at every place of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,M'$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ at which `jq` has non-negative order, and such that the $R_0$-residue of $g$ lies in the valuation subring of the place $s$, the following holds: whenever the coefficientwise image of $g$ in $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,k_0$ lies in $K$ and, as an element of $K$, lies in $C$, and whenever $c \in k_0$ lies in $A$ and has residue in $A$ equal to $s.\mathrm{evalAt}$ applied to the $R_0$-residue of $g$, the difference in $C$ between that element and the image of $c$ lies in $y$ (respectively in $y'$).
--
--   Conclusion. There exist $\gamma \in \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb Z)$ with $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$ and $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$, and a $k_0$-algebra automorphism $\tau$ of $K$, such that:
--
--   (i) $\mathrm{IsLevelAutAt}\,k_0\,q\,\xi\,q\,(q^2M')\,H_1\,\gamma^{-1}\,K\,\tau$ holds: for every weight $k \in \mathbb Z$, all modular forms $f, g$ of weight $k$ for $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$ viewed in $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb R)$, all integral power series $p_f, p_g$ which are the $q$-expansions of $f$ and $g$ with $p_g$ giving a nonzero Laurent series over $\mathbb Q$, every $x \in K$ whose Laurent expansion is the coefficientwise image of the quotient of the series of $p_f$ by that of $p_g$, and every ring homomorphism $\iota : k_0 \to \mathbb C$ with $\iota(\xi) = e^{2\pi i/q}$, the coefficientwise image under $\iota$ of $\tau x$ multiplied by the $q$-expansion of $g \mid_k \mathrm{conjElemN}\,q\,\gamma^{-1}$ equals the $q$-expansion of $f \mid_k \mathrm{conjElemN}\,q\,\gamma^{-1}$, where $\mathrm{conjElemN}\,q\,\delta$ is the matrix $\begin{pmatrix} \delta_{00} & \delta_{01}/q \\ q\,\delta_{10} & \delta_{11}\end{pmatrix}$;
--
--   (ii) $\tau$ maps $C$ into $C$, i.e. $\tau f \in C$ for every $f \in C$;
--
--   (iii) for every $f \in C$ with $\tau f \in C$, one has $f \in y$ if and only if $\tau f \in y'$.
--
--   This is the transitivity step for the integral chart of the level-$H_1$ curve above a supersingular place $s$ in characteristic $q = 2$: any two maximal ideals of the integral closure of $A_0[j]$ in $K$ that reduce functions at $s$ in the same way are interchanged by a level automorphism attached to an element of $\Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$, which moreover preserves the chart. It is the $q = 2$, $\Gamma_1(\ell_g)$-diamond variant of the corresponding orbit statement at larger $q$, and is used in the construction of the supersingular fibre dictionary and in the assembly of the semistable chart, nodes and inertia data at level $q^2M'$.
